														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2166780">
									

<p>I disagree; the traditional 'Ghost Train' runs between specific points, and is visible only under certain circumstances. An example is the Funeral train that runs from DC to Springfield, IL, on (or around) the anniversary of Lincoln's death &#8212; it is not seen by all, watches/clocks/etc., stop as it goes by, and it's only around for a short while during a specific time each year.</p>
<p>This, on the other hand, as befits an SCP, obeys some kind of scientifically definable (even if not understood) rules, and its behavior is consistent and predictable, within the parameters of those rules. We may not understand where it came from, nor why it obeys its rules, but they are consistent, and its behavior may be accurately predicted using the identified rules.</p>
<p>Were it just a 'True-blue completely straight depiction of a ghost train' there would be no controlling / predicting /etc.</p>
<p>Incidentally, I would have guessed it's origin to have been the Salisbury Rail crash in 1906, but that was an L12 class, not a Minerva. (Not easily mistaken for each other, either; the L12 is a 4-4-0, the Minerva a 2-4-0.) There were a few LSWR crashes in the late 1800s, but the Salisbury crash had the most loss of life.</p>
<p>*Disclaimer &#8212; I've been working for a Railroad for 17 years; I've heard a LOT of train stories, legends, myths, and so forth.*</p>
<p>Upvoted :)</p>

